<!-- HIGH VALUE OPTIONS -->

<section class="container">

    <h2 class="section-title">Career Paths to Investigate</h2>

    <p class="section-description">
        Do not automatically choose the highest-paying field.
        Choose the field where your interests, abilities, available training,
        job demand, and evidence-building opportunities overlap.
    </p>

    <div class="grid">

        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">Cloud & Infrastructure</div>

            <div class="card-body">

                <p>
                    A pathway for people interested in systems, infrastructure,
                    cloud platforms, and technical problem solving.
                </p>

                <ul>
                    <li>Cloud fundamentals</li>
                    <li>Networking</li>
                    <li>Linux</li>
                    <li>Identity and access</li>
                    <li>Cloud administration</li>
                </ul>

                <strong>Potential next steps:</strong>
                <p>Cloud fundamentals → projects → junior cloud/IT roles</p>

            </div>
        </div>


        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">Cybersecurity</div>

            <div class="card-body">

                <p>
                    A field involving security controls, monitoring,
                    risk management, systems, and investigation.
                </p>

                <ul>
                    <li>Security fundamentals</li>
                    <li>Networking</li>
                    <li>Identity management</li>
                    <li>Security monitoring</li>
                    <li>Risk management</li>
                </ul>

                <strong>Potential next steps:</strong>
                <p>IT fundamentals → security fundamentals → labs → entry-level security/IT</p>

            </div>
        </div>


        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">Web Development</div>

            <div class="card-body">

                <p>
                    A portfolio-driven field where projects can demonstrate
                    ability before professional experience is extensive.
                </p>

                <ul>
                    <li>HTML</li>
                    <li>CSS</li>
                    <li>JavaScript</li>
                    <li>Git/GitHub</li>
                    <li>Responsive design</li>
                </ul>

                <strong>Potential next steps:</strong>
                <p>Learn fundamentals → build projects → portfolio → freelance/junior roles</p>

            </div>
        </div>


        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">Data Analytics</div>

            <div class="card-body">

                <p>
                    A pathway for people who enjoy numbers, patterns,
                    business questions, and explaining findings.
                </p>

                <ul>
                    <li>Excel</li>
                    <li>SQL</li>
                    <li>Data visualization</li>
                    <li>Statistics</li>
                    <li>Business analysis</li>
                </ul>

                <strong>Potential next steps:</strong>
                <p>Excel → SQL → dashboards → portfolio → analyst applications</p>

            </div>
        </div>


        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">CRM & Business Systems</div>

            <div class="card-body">

                <p>
                    A strong option for people who enjoy business processes,
                    customer data, organization, automation, and technology.
                </p>

                <ul>
                    <li>CRM administration</li>
                    <li>Sales operations</li>
                    <li>Customer data</li>
                    <li>Workflow automation</li>
                    <li>Reporting</li>
                </ul>

                <strong>Potential next steps:</strong>
                <p>CRM fundamentals → sandbox projects → portfolio → operations/CRM roles</p>

            </div>
        </div>


        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">Digital Marketing</div>

            <div class="card-body">

                <p>
                    A practical path for people interested in customer behavior,
                    content, advertising, analytics, and business growth.
                </p>

                <ul>
                    <li>SEO</li>
                    <li>Email marketing</li>
                    <li>Paid advertising</li>
                    <li>Analytics</li>
                    <li>Content strategy</li>
                </ul>

                <strong>Potential next steps:</strong>
                <p>Learn → create campaigns → produce case studies → apply to marketing roles</p>

            </div>
        </div>


        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">UX/UI Design</div>

            <div class="card-body">

                <p>
                    A design-focused field involving user research,
                    interfaces, usability, and product experiences.
                </p>

                <ul>
                    <li>Wireframes</li>
                    <li>User research</li>
                    <li>Interface design</li>
                    <li>Prototyping</li>
                    <li>Usability testing</li>
                </ul>

                <strong>Potential next steps:</strong>
                <p>Design fundamentals → 3 case studies → portfolio → applications</p>

            </div>
        </div>


        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">Project Coordination</div>

            <div class="card-body">

                <p>
                    A potential entry point for organized people who enjoy
                    planning, communication, tracking, and execution.
                </p>

                <ul>
                    <li>Project planning</li>
                    <li>Task tracking</li>
                    <li>Documentation</li>
                    <li>Stakeholder communication</li>
                    <li>Process improvement</li>
                </ul>

                <strong>Potential next steps:</strong>
                <p>Project fundamentals → sample project → project portfolio → coordinator roles</p>

            </div>
        </div>

    </div>

</section>


<!-- STRATEGY -->

<section class="container strategy" id="strategy">

    <h2 class="section-title">The Sample Before You Commit Strategy</h2>

    <p class="section-description">
        Before spending hundreds or thousands of dollars, test the career
        like an experiment. Your goal is to gather evidence before making
        a large commitment.
    </p>

    <div class="strategy-grid">

        <div class="strategy-card">
            <div class="strategy-number">1</div>
            <h3>Study the Jobs First</h3>
            <p>
                Look at 20–30 real job postings. Record repeated skills,
                software, certifications, experience requirements, salary
                ranges, and job titles.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="strategy-card">
            <div class="strategy-number">2</div>
            <h3>Sample the Skill</h3>
            <p>
                Spend 7–14 days learning the basics through free resources.
                Do not buy an expensive program yet.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="strategy-card">
            <div class="strategy-number">3</div>
            <h3>Build Something</h3>
            <p>
                Create a small project that demonstrates the skill.
                Employers should be able to see evidence rather than only
                seeing a certificate.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="strategy-card">
            <div class="strategy-number">4</div>
            <h3>Test the Market</h3>
            <p>
                Begin applying to appropriate positions while continuing
                to learn. Measure responses rather than assuming your
                strategy is working.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="strategy-card">
            <div class="strategy-number">5</div>
            <h3>Improve the Weak Link</h3>
            <p>
                If applications receive no response, investigate the resume,
                job targeting, experience gap, portfolio, interview ability,
                or certification relevance.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="strategy-card">
            <div class="strategy-number">6</div>
            <h3>Only Then Invest</h3>
            <p>
                Pay for a certification or structured program when your
                evidence suggests the credential will solve an actual gap.
            </p>
        </div>

    </div>

</section>


<!-- TIMELINE -->

<section class="container timeline-section">

    <h2 class="section-title">12-Week Career Testing Timeline</h2>

    <p class="section-description">
        This is a sample framework. The exact timeline can change depending
        on the field, your available time, and the complexity of the skill.
    </p>

    <div class="timeline">

        <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="timeline-dot"></div>

            <div class="timeline-content">
                <h3>Week 1 — Career Research</h3>

                <p>
                    Choose 2–3 possible career paths. Examine approximately
                    20–30 job postings for each path.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>Deliverable:</strong> A list of the 5–10 most
                    frequently requested skills.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>


        <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="timeline-dot"></div>

            <div class="timeline-content">
                <h3>Weeks 2–3 — Skill Sampling</h3>

                <p>
                    Use free resources to experience the actual work.
                    Complete beginner exercises rather than watching videos
                    passively.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>Deliverable:</strong> One small completed project.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>


        <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="timeline-dot"></div>

            <div class="timeline-content">
                <h3>Weeks 4–5 — Evidence Building</h3>

                <p>
                    Build one stronger project that resembles something an
                    employer might actually ask an employee to do.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>Deliverable:</strong> Portfolio project,
                    case study, GitHub repository, dashboard, campaign,
                    documentation, or other appropriate proof.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>


        <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="timeline-dot"></div>

            <div class="timeline-content">
                <h3>Weeks 6–7 — Resume & Application Testing</h3>

                <p>
                    Create a targeted resume and begin applying to realistic
                    positions. Do not wait until you feel completely ready.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>Measure:</strong> Applications sent,
                    responses, recruiter messages, assessments, and interviews.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>


        <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="timeline-dot"></div>

            <div class="timeline-content">
                <h3>Weeks 8–9 — Certification Decision</h3>

                <p>
                    Now ask whether a certification actually fills a gap
                    identified in job postings or employer feedback.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>Decision:</strong> Continue free learning,
                    purchase targeted training, or change direction.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>


        <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="timeline-dot"></div>

            <div class="timeline-content">
                <h3>Weeks 10–12 — Market Validation</h3>

                <p>
                    Continue applying while improving your evidence and
                    interviewing skills.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>Goal:</strong> Determine whether the combination
                    of skill + evidence + resume + interview preparation is
                    producing better opportunities.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

    </div>

</section>


<!-- SCORECARD -->

<section class="container score-section">

    <h2 class="section-title">Track Whether the Strategy Is Working</h2>

    <p class="section-description">
        Do not judge your career strategy based on feelings alone.
        Track the funnel from applications to interviews.
    </p>

    <div class="scorecard">

        <table>

            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Metric</th>
                    <th>What It Tells You</th>
                    <th>Warning Sign</th>
                    <th>Possible Adjustment</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>

                <tr>
                    <td>Applications</td>
                    <td>How much effort is entering the pipeline</td>
                    <td>Very few applications</td>
                    <td>Increase targeted applications</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>Employer Responses</td>
                    <td>Whether your profile is attracting attention</td>
                    <td>Almost no responses</td>
                    <td>Improve targeting, resume, evidence, or qualifications</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>Interviews</td>
                    <td>Whether your application converts into conversations</td>
                    <td>Many applications but few interviews</td>
                    <td>Investigate resume, experience, portfolio, and job fit</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>Interview Progression</td>
                    <td>Whether interviews turn into later stages</td>
                    <td>Repeated rejection after interviews</td>
                    <td>Improve interview preparation and technical examples</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>Offers</td>
                    <td>Whether the entire strategy produces employment</td>
                    <td>No offers after substantial testing</td>
                    <td>Reassess role targeting, skills, evidence, and market demand</td>
                </tr>

            </tbody>

        </table>

    </div>

</section>


<!-- FAILURE STRATEGY -->

<section class="container failure">

    <h2 class="section-title">What If the Certification Doesn't Work?</h2>

    <p class="section-description">
        A certification not producing interviews does not automatically mean
        you failed. It means you need to determine which part of the system
        is failing.
    </p>

    <div class="failure-box">

        <h3>Do Not Immediately Buy Another Certification</h3>

        <p>
            Repeatedly purchasing credentials without diagnosing the problem
            can create a cycle of spending money while the underlying issue
            remains unchanged.
        </p>

        <div class="failure-grid">

            <div class="failure-card">

                <h4>Problem: No Interviews</h4>

                <p>
                    If you are submitting many targeted applications and
                    receiving almost no interviews, examine the resume,
                    job targeting, experience level, keywords, portfolio,
                    and whether the credential is actually requested.
                </p>

            </div>


            <div class="failure-card">

                <h4>Problem: Interviews But No Offers</h4>

                <p>
                    The credential may have done its job by getting you into
                    the conversation. The next bottleneck may be interviewing,
                    technical demonstrations, communication, or insufficient
                    examples of past work.
                </p>

            </div>


            <div class="failure-card">

                <h4>Problem: Employers Want Experience</h4>

                <p>
                    Build experience substitutes: projects, volunteer work,
                    freelance work, nonprofit projects, internships,
                    simulations, documentation, and realistic portfolio work.
                </p>

            </div>


            <div class="failure-card">

                <h4>Problem: Certification Isn't Requested</h4>

                <p>
                    Stop treating the certification as the centerpiece.
                    Shift toward the skills and evidence repeatedly requested
                    in actual job postings.
                </p>

            </div>


            <div class="failure-card">

                <h4>Problem: Market Is Too Competitive</h4>

                <p>
                    Consider adjacent roles where your existing skills are
                    more useful. A related entry point can sometimes be more
                    realistic than attempting to enter the most competitive
                    role immediately.
                </p>

            </div>


            <div class="failure-card">

                <h4>Problem: You Dislike the Work</h4>

                <p>
                    Treat that as valuable information. Do not spend months
                    forcing yourself into a career simply because someone
                    said it pays well.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>

</section>


<!-- DECISION TREE -->

<section class="container decision">

    <h2 class="section-title">Your Continue, Pivot, or Stop Decision</h2>

    <p class="section-description">
        After a meaningful testing period, make a deliberate decision instead
        of endlessly accumulating courses.
    </p>

    <div class="decision-grid">

        <div class="decision-card keep">

            <h3>CONTINUE</h3>

            <p>
                Continue when you see evidence that the path is improving.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Interview opportunities are increasing</li>
                <li>Your skills are improving</li>
                <li>Employers recognize your evidence</li>
                <li>You enjoy enough of the work to continue</li>
                <li>You can identify a clear next skill</li>
            </ul>

        </div>


        <div class="decision-card pivot">

            <h3>PIVOT</h3>

            <p>
                Change your approach when the career may still be viable
                but your current entry strategy is weak.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Applications produce little response</li>
                <li>Your target roles require experience you don't have</li>
                <li>An adjacent role is more accessible</li>
                <li>You need stronger projects</li>
                <li>You need a different specialization</li>
            </ul>

        </div>


        <div class="decision-card stop">

            <h3>STOP</h3>

            <p>
                Stop investing heavily when evidence consistently indicates
                the path is a poor fit.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>You dislike the actual work</li>
                <li>The training is financially damaging</li>
                <li>The credential isn't relevant to target jobs</li>
                <li>Multiple strategy changes produce no improvement</li>
                <li>A different path has substantially stronger evidence</li>
            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>

</section>


<!-- EMERGENCY CAREER PLAN -->

<section class="container checklist">

    <h2 class="section-title">If Money Is Tight, Use This Order</h2>

    <ul>

        <li>
            Research the job market before buying training.
        </li>

        <li>
            Use free learning resources first.
        </li>

        <li>
            Build one small project before buying a major program.
        </li>

        <li>
            Begin applying when you have enough evidence to demonstrate beginner ability.
        </li>

        <li>
            Track applications, responses, interviews, and rejections.
        </li>

        <li>
            Fix the bottleneck before purchasing another credential.
        </li>

        <li>
            Prefer targeted credentials requested by employers over collecting unrelated certificates.
        </li>

        <li>
            Build experience through projects, volunteer work, freelance work,
            apprenticeships, internships, contract work, or practical simulations.
        </li>

        <li>
            Keep one career path as the primary experiment and one adjacent
            path as a backup rather than constantly starting over.
        </li>

    </ul>

</section>


<!-- CTA -->

<section class="cta">

    <h2>Build a Career Experiment, Not a Certification Collection</h2>

    <p>
        Your objective is not to accumulate the most certificates.
        Your objective is to become more employable. Learn the skill,
        produce evidence, test the market, measure the results, identify
        the bottleneck, and change your strategy when the evidence tells you to.
    </p>

</section>


